+# BLOCKED: exact streaming median in constant memory
+
+## Requirement
+
+`test_constant_memory` requires `median()` to consume a one-pass generator of
+200,000 arbitrary floats and return the exact median while allocating less than
+64 KB of Python memory (as measured by `tracemalloc`).
+
+## Why it cannot be met
+
+The two requirements are mutually exclusive for a general one-pass stream:
+
+- **Exactness requires retaining order information.** Finding the exact median
+  of an arbitrary sequence in a single pass requires Ω(n) space. Picking the
+  middle element is a selection problem; no constant-space algorithm can
+  distinguish all possible streams well enough to name the exact middle value.
+  Approximate streaming medians exist precisely because exact ones are
+  impossible under a bounded-memory, single-pass constraint.
+- **The test's own data already exceeds the budget.** The feed contains
+  200,000 arbitrary `float64` values. Merely holding those values requires
+  `200,000 * 8 = 1,600,000` bytes, about 24 times the 64 KB limit, before any
+  Python object or algorithm overhead. The observed peak with the exact
+  implementation is 7,221,928 bytes.
+
+The generator is consumed once (`values` cannot be replayed), so the usual
+alternative -- repeated passes with constant auxiliary storage -- is also
+unavailable.
+
+Satisfying the test would therefore require one of the following, all of which
+change the contract:
+
+1. Return an approximation and relax the exactness assertion.
+2. Restrict inputs to a known, small value domain and count values.
+3. Spill to disk / use an external sort, making memory constant but adding
+   O(n) disk usage and I/O.
+4. Exploit details of the test's seeded generator rather than the values
+   actually passed to `median()`, which is not a real solution.
+
+None of these is an honest implementation of the stated requirement, so no
+workaround was applied. The test is left in place and continues to fail on its
+memory assertion.
+
+## What was completed
+
+- Fixed the even-count bug: `median([1, 2, 3, 4])` now returns `2.5` (the mean
+  of the middle two) instead of `3`.
+- Preserved exactness, empty-input handling, and one-pass generator support;
+  the implementation continues to use O(n) memory.
+- Test status: `test_odd`, `test_even`, `test_empty_raises`, and
+  `test_generator_input` pass. `test_constant_memory` returns the correct
+  median but fails with a peak of 7,221,928 bytes.
+
+## Decision needed
+
+Choose which requirement to relax: exactness (use a bounded-memory approximate
+quantile algorithm), memory/disk model (use external sorting), or input model
+(require a replayable/bounded-domain source). Once the contract is clear,
+`median()` can be implemented accordingly.
